1
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:28,020
Virkka Hanttu from T4F Finland.

2
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:29,760
This is Pro's Guide:

3
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:32,160
Dynamic CQP Fundamentals.

4
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:33,480
In this episode,

5
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:36,000
we are going through team

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,160
movement, concentrating on

7
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:40,680
corridors, and movement from room

8
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:41,520
to room.

9
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:43,920
The team rules are like traffic

10
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:45,840
rules: they tell you where you

11
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:47,460
drive, where you go,

12
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:49,560
how you group when you turn.

13
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:51,000
The team members have to be able

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,700
to read each other so they can

15
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:55,320
predict what's going to happen

16
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:56,820
next, even though they don't see

17
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:58,500
in the rooms.

18
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:01,260
As a principle told earlier,

19
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:02,760
the point man always takes the

20
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:04,080
biggest threat.

21
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:06,000
Would it be the open door in the

22
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:07,500
end of the corridor,

23
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:08,940
or would it be the tangle?

24
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:11,940
Second man supports has overall

25
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:13,140
security.

26
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:14,700
We have a saying:

27
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:17,280
number one takes the door,

28
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:20,100
number two takes the long.

29
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:22,680
This means, on the corridor,

30
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:25,320
number one concentrates on the

31
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:27,900
open door and what's behind it,

32
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:30,600
and number two provides security

33
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:32,160
on the corridor.

34
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:34,200
Everybody else in the stack

35
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:36,240
supports the actions of number

36
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:38,580
one and number two.

37
00:01:38,580 --> 00:01:40,140
Then we have a few basic

38
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:42,000
fundamental rules on team

39
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:42,900
movement.

40
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:43,980
First one of them is:

41
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:45,600
don't fight from the tunnels.

42
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:47,640
Would it be a doorframe or a

43
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:48,660
corridor?

44
00:01:48,660 --> 00:01:50,520
Always, when you go into one,

45
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:53,760
have a plan where you go next.

46
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:55,860
So when you enter a corridor,

47
00:01:55,860 --> 00:01:57,180
have a plan:

48
00:01:57,180 --> 00:01:58,860
which door are you taking,

49
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:00,480
what are your next actions,

50
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:02,640
so you don't have to stop at the

51
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:03,660
corridor.

52
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:06,480
Always have a gun pointing on an

53
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:08,100
opening angle.

54
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:10,020
This means, if the point man is

55
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:11,520
going and there are angles

56
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:13,500
opening, have a gun pointing at

57
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:14,640
the opening angles.

58
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:17,580
Would it be opposing door or

59
00:02:17,580 --> 00:02:18,300
T-junction?

60
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:20,640
Always have a gun pointing to the

61
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:22,380
opening angle.

62
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:24,780
Number one man is always right.

63
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:25,980
This meaning, even though he

64
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:28,200
doesn't make good tactical

65
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:30,660
decisions, we don't make a

66
00:02:30,660 --> 00:02:32,220
mistake over a mistake,

67
00:02:32,220 --> 00:02:34,260
so then you correct it.

68
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:35,760
In this line of work,

69
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:37,620
your life depends on it,

70
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:39,720
so you want everything to be as

71
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:41,220
good as it can get.

72
00:02:41,220 --> 00:02:43,080
So we support each other.

73
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:44,820
So everybody concentrates on

74
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:47,100
their own tasks.

75
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:48,660
This also means that the number

76
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:51,000
one man has priority.

77
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:52,860
If he wants to go left,

78
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:54,300
everybody else follows him

79
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:56,160
because he doesn't look back.

80
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:57,780
The guys are supporting.

81
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:00,120
He has to be able to concentrate

82
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:02,280
on his own work.

83
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:04,200
He has the best relative

84
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:05,940
situational awareness,

85
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:08,580
so he decides where the stack is

86
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:09,780
going.

87
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:11,580
He doesn't look back because the

88
00:03:11,580 --> 00:03:13,260
guys are on his heels,

89
00:03:13,260 --> 00:03:15,420
and when their train starts,

90
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:16,740
they will follow.

91
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:19,860
So now we will start by doing

92
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:22,320
four-man stack corridor movement.

93
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:24,600
So guys, load and make ready.

94
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:26,400
First room on the right,

95
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:28,560
last room on the left.

96
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:31,680
Good procedure on the corridor.

97
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:33,300
We go live.

98
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:40,620
Questions?

99
00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:58,020
All right.

100
00:03:58,020 --> 00:04:00,780
Let's go again.

101
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:02,460
That was just a little bit

102
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:47,820
froggy.

103
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:49,380
Good movement on the corridor.

104
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:51,120
Number one has the open door,

105
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:52,320
number two has the long,

106
00:04:52,320 --> 00:05:04,980
and that's how you build it up.

107
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:07,860
Good movement on the corridor.

108
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:10,020
Priorities.

109
00:05:10,020 --> 00:05:11,640
Number one has the target,

110
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:13,560
number two has long.

111
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:16,500
Good shots before the threshold.

112
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:17,880
Corners for corners.

113
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:19,080
Well done.

114
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:22,260
Next exercise will be covering

115
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:23,580
while moving.

116
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:25,680
So if there are openings,

117
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:27,420
opening up when the number one is

118
00:05:27,420 --> 00:05:28,560
going.

119
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:30,600
Number two man, three man,

120
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:32,820
they take the responsibility to

121
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:35,160
put themselves and their gun

122
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:37,740
between the number one's back and

123
00:05:37,740 --> 00:05:39,660
the opening threat.

124
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:41,700
We call this bunkering.

125
00:05:41,700 --> 00:05:42,960
This is how it goes.

126
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:55,920
Gentlemen, start.

127
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:58,680
So here, the first man is going

128
00:05:58,680 --> 00:05:59,940
in.

129
00:05:59,940 --> 00:06:02,460
The second man is bunkering him,

130
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:04,740
so providing cover with his gun

131
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:07,860
and blades to his back.

132
00:06:07,860 --> 00:06:10,440
The rest of the stack will go in,

133
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:12,240
and the bunkering man is the last

134
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:13,260
one to go in.

135
00:06:13,260 --> 00:07:10,020
All right, continue.

136
00:07:10,020 --> 00:07:11,100
Now we are applying the

137
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:13,620
principles of footwork,

138
00:07:13,620 --> 00:07:16,260
decision making, and shooting for

139
00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:18,480
the rooms in the darkness using

140
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:20,400
NVGs.

141
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:23,220
Again, nothing changes.

142
00:07:23,220 --> 00:07:25,080
First exercise will be one-man

143
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:26,580
entry.

144
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:28,980
Just footwork, decision making,

145
00:07:28,980 --> 00:07:30,780
good shots.

146
00:07:30,780 --> 00:07:33,060
Load and make ready.

147
00:07:33,060 --> 00:09:01,200
Move.

148
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:03,540
Next exercise will be NVG

149
00:09:03,540 --> 00:09:04,980
corridor movement.

150
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:06,240
Nothing changes.

151
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:06,480
Go.
